  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 1213 AM MDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\nFriday and Saturday, the area is forecast to be stuck in the \nmiddle of multiple features with an upper trough over the \nWestern United States and Northern Plains, while a hurricane \npushes into the Ohio River Valley. In spite of all these \nfeatures in the United States, this should leave the area in a \nrelatively stagnant pattern with a surface low hanging around \nthe High and Northern Plains that extends into the area. This \nshould keep southerly low level flow and dry air place over the \narea, allowing temperatures to warm into the mid 80s to lower \n90s. The dry air and warm temperatures are forecast to have \nrelatively humidity lower into the teens and 20s during the \nafternoon, with the lowest values for counties along the \nColorado border. With the pressure gradient over the area \nkeeping winds around 15-20 mph and gusts around 30 mph, there is\nincreasing concern for at least a few hours of critical fire \nweather conditions. But with ensemble spread charts showing most\nof the features could either shift or become more broad, don't \nhave the confidence to issue any products yet as conditions \ncould still change a bit. One of the things I am more confident \nin is the lack of precipitation. While we have features \nnear/over the area, the air will likely be too dry for anything \nto form, keeping our precip chances under 5%.\n\nSunday and early next week remain a mess with two northern \nupper troughs possible along the the upper low over the Western \nUnited States potentially moving near the area. For now, it is \nlikely that we will have westerly/southwesterly flow with all \nthese features that should bring some mid to high level moisture\ninto the area. The increased cloud cover and influence of the \nupper troughs trying to push the surface low east and allow for \ncold air advection should lead to cooler temperatures for that \nstart of the week. For now, the forecast is that temperatures \nlower each day by about 5 degrees with highs forecast to be in \nthe mid 60s be mid-week. With the uncertainty in how strong each\nfeature will be and when it will advance, some ensemble \nsolutions suggest much colder air would be possible. Even so, \nthe chance for freezing temperatures is only around 5-10%. This \nhas also severely lower the snow chances hinted at by prior runs\nin guidance. What hasn't lowered is the chance for \nprecipitation with the influx of mid and upper level moisture. \nWhile unlikely to be widespread or lead to decent accumulations \ndue to dry near surface air, Mon-Wed has a 30-50% chance of rain\neach day.",
